        </section>


        <!-- FAILURE MECHANISM -->

        <section>
          <div class="container">
            <p class="section-label">08 — Failure Mechanism</p>

            <h2>
              Connecting the physical defect to a possible electrical ignition mechanism.
            </h2>

            <p class="section-intro">
              The evidence did not establish a single observed ignition event.
              Instead, the investigation evaluated whether the physical
              condition of the connector could produce known electrical
              failure modes capable of generating heat.
            </p>

            <div class="failure-flow">
              <div class="flow-item">
                <div class="flow-number">01</div>
                <strong>Defective Connection</strong>
                <p>
                  Ground conductor disconnected and connector pins displaced.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="flow-item">
                <div class="flow-number">02</div>
                <strong>Abnormal Electrical Path</strong>
                <p>
                  Loss of a stable return path or unintended contact between
                  conductors.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="flow-item">
                <div class="flow-number">03</div>
                <strong>Arcing or Short Circuit</strong>
                <p>
                  Electrical energy could be dissipated through an unintended
                  current path.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div class="flow-item">
                <div class="flow-number">04</div>
                <strong>Localized Heating</strong>
                <p>
                  Heat or sparks could potentially ignite nearby cable
                  materials or surrounding combustibles.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>


        <!-- CONCLUSION -->

        <section>
          <div class="container">
            <p class="section-label">09 — Engineering Conclusion</p>

            <h2>
              A technically supported explanation — with the uncertainty preserved.
            </h2>

            <div class="conclusion">
              <div class="conclusion-label">Conclusion</div>

              <p>
                The combined fire-scene evidence, X-ray imaging, and exemplar
                disassembly supported the defective pink USB-to-Lightning
                cable as a plausible ignition source. The displaced connector
                pins and disconnected ground conductor created conditions
                that could potentially produce electrical arcing or a
                short circuit and the localized heating associated with
                those failure modes.
              </p>

              <p class="qualification">
                Forensic engineering requires distinguishing what the
                evidence supports from what it cannot prove. The investigation
                identified a technically plausible failure mechanism, but did
                not claim direct observation of the initiating electrical event.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>


        <!-- LEARNING -->

        <section>
          <div class="container">
            <p class="section-label">10 — What I Learned</p>

            <h2>
              Engineering from evidence instead of a known starting condition.
            </h2>

            <p class="section-intro">
              This project required a different engineering mindset from
              traditional design work. Instead of creating a system and
              measuring its behavior, I had to reconstruct what may have
              happened from incomplete and fire-damaged physical evidence.
              The process strengthened my ability to compare competing
              hypotheses, connect physical observations to electrical failure
              mechanisms, and communicate conclusions without overstating
              what the available evidence could establish.
            </p>

